\description{
Tests for differences between two or more populations of brain networks,
using the L1-distance ANOVA statistic T of Fraiman and Fraiman (2018)
and a permutation null distribution. Valid inference requires exchangeable
group labels under the null; equal central graphs alone are not enough.
This is the global test
that \code{\link{identify_critical_links}} runs before it starts removing
edges; here it is available on its own, so that the population-level
question can be answered, and reported, before any edge is singled out.
}
\details{
The statistic T contrasts the mean distance of each graph to its
  own population's central graph with its mean distance to every
  population's central graph (see \code{\link{compute_test_statistic}}).
  The test uses the lower tail of T and does not use a normal approximation.
  Replicates are generated by
  permuting the group labels across the pooled sample while preserving the
  group sizes, T is recomputed for each, and
  \eqn{p = (1 + \#\{b : T_b \le T_{obs}\}) / (B + 1)}, which is never below
  \eqn{1 / (B + 1)}. The combined criterion is described in
  \code{\link{identify_critical_links}}.

  Group labels must be exchangeable under the null: shuffling whole graphs
  must preserve the joint distribution of the observations. Independent
  graphs drawn from the same graph distribution satisfy this condition.
  Matching edge means alone does not, because dependence between edges can
  differ. Unrestricted permutations do not support paired observations,
  repeated measures, blocks or adjustment for covariates.

  The normalisation constant \code{a} of
  \code{\link{compute_test_statistic}} is fixed at 1. The \eqn{p} value
  does not depend on it: \code{a} rescales the observed statistic and every
  replicate by the same positive factor, which leaves their comparison
  unchanged.

  Distances sum all matrix entries. For symmetric graphs with zero diagonal,
  this counts each undirected edge twice, so reported T is twice the value
  from an upper-triangle-only distance. This shared positive factor does
  not change the permutation p-value.

  The computation uses the edge-wise decomposition of T described in
  \code{\link{identify_critical_links}}: the statistic of every replicate
  is obtained from permuted edge counts through matrix products,
  rather than by rebuilding graphs and recomputing distances. That
  decomposition holds for binary graphs, which is why the input must be
  binary. Calling \code{global_test()} and
  \code{\link{identify_critical_links}} with the same populations,
  \code{n_permutations} and \code{seed} evaluates the same permutations, so
  the \eqn{p} value returned here is the one that
  \code{identify_critical_links()} records in its \code{p_value} component.
}
\section{Equal group sizes}{

  The current T test requires the same number of graphs in every group.
  With unequal group sizes, a real difference can move T away from the
  rejection tail. Such designs are rejected until a revised test is
  validated. The package does not drop observations to balance groups.
}

